WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.760
Welcome back.

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.340
In straight line motion, we described how velocity changes.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.640
Today we ask why it changes.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.320
Imagine pushing a box across a floor.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.440
Before writing an equation, choose the box as the body you are studying.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.340
Draw its weight downward, the floor's normal reaction upward, your push to the

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.780
right, and friction to the left if the box tends to slide right.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.900
This is a free body diagram.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.860
Every arrow must name a real interaction.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.680
Do not draw an extra arrow called motion or acceleration.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.440
Acceleration is the result we calculate.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:36.860
Now imagine the forces balance.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:38.760
Must the box be stationary?

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.380
Take a moment to think.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:41.300
No.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.440
Newton's first law says zero net force means constant velocity in an inertial

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:46.680
frame.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.680
That constant velocity may be zero, but it does not have to be.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.340
For ordinary classroom problems we usually treat the ground as approximately

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:55.860
inertial.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.680
A useful way to test your diagram is to ask who is exerting each force.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:02.240
Earth supplies weight.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.340
The floor supplies contact forces.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.300
Your hand supplies the push.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:11.060
In JEE problems, getting this picture right often matters more than the algebra

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:11.740
that follows.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.680
Newton's second law connects the diagram to motion.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.740
For a constant mass, the vector sum of external forces equals mass times

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:21.340
acceleration.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.540
Notice the word sum.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.880
One force alone does not tell you the acceleration when several forces act.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:29.760
Choose right as positive.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:35.660
A five kilogram box has a fourteen newton pull to the right and four newtons of

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:36.360
friction to the left.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.440
Before calculating, predict the direction.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:41.380
The rightward force wins.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.680
The net force is fourteen minus four, or ten newtons to the right.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.540
Divide by five kilograms to get two metres per second squared.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.620
We subtract because the forces oppose each other.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.100
Now separate the vertical direction.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:59.320
If the box has no vertical acceleration, and only weight and the normal

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.800
reaction act vertically, the normal reaction equals its weight.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.800
That conclusion follows from the vertical equation.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.740
It is not a universal rule.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.540
An upward angled pull would reduce the floor's required reaction.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.520
A lift accelerating upward would change it too.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:20.340
Here is your exam routine: draw the body, choose axes, resolve forces, add

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.600
signed components, then divide by mass.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.381
Check that the acceleration points in the direction of the net force, even when

00:02:26.381 --> 00:02:26.784
the body is currently moving the other way.

00:02:26.784 --> 00:02:31.904
If every force has an equal and opposite partner, why does anything accelerate?

00:02:32.744 --> 00:02:34.484
The answer is in the body you selected.

00:02:35.224 --> 00:02:39.604
When your hand pushes a box, the box pushes your hand with equal magnitude and

00:02:39.604 --> 00:02:40.464
opposite direction.

00:02:41.184 --> 00:02:43.744
The force on the box belongs on the box's diagram.

00:02:44.344 --> 00:02:47.024
The force on your hand belongs on your hand's diagram.

00:02:47.684 --> 00:02:50.064
They do not cancel in the net force on the box alone.

00:02:50.864 --> 00:02:52.364
Try a common JEE trap.

00:02:52.824 --> 00:02:54.204
A book rests on a table.

00:02:54.204 --> 00:02:58.064
Its weight points downward and the normal reaction points upward.

00:02:58.864 --> 00:03:00.004
Are these a third law pair?

00:03:01.004 --> 00:03:03.104
Think about which bodies receive the forces.

00:03:04.084 --> 00:03:07.484
Both forces act on the book, so they are not partners under the third law.

00:03:08.324 --> 00:03:11.424
The partner of Earth's gravitational pull on the book is the book's

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:12.624
gravitational pull on Earth.

00:03:13.024 --> 00:03:16.904
The partner of the table's contact force on the book is the book's contact

00:03:16.904 --> 00:03:18.524
force on the table.

00:03:18.524 --> 00:03:21.244
Equal magnitudes do not by themselves identify a pair.

00:03:21.764 --> 00:03:24.304
Name the interaction and reverse the giver and receiver.

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.224
That is the reliable test.

00:03:26.924 --> 00:03:31.244
The two forces act simultaneously; the words action and reaction do not mean

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:32.484
that one force happens later.

00:03:33.648 --> 00:03:38.308
Friction is where a correct formula can still give a wrong answer if you choose

00:03:38.308 --> 00:03:39.328
the wrong situation.

00:03:40.488 --> 00:03:43.988
Static friction adjusts to prevent relative slipping, up to a limit.

00:03:44.668 --> 00:03:46.568
It does not always equal that limit.

00:03:47.908 --> 00:03:52.068
Consider a four kilogram block initially at rest on a horizontal floor.

00:03:52.708 --> 00:03:56.888
Use g equal to ten metres per second squared, as specified in this example.

00:03:57.588 --> 00:03:59.748
The normal reaction is forty newtons.

00:03:59.748 --> 00:04:03.928
Let the static friction coefficient be zero point five and the kinetic

00:04:03.928 --> 00:04:05.568
coefficient zero point three.

00:04:06.308 --> 00:04:08.568
The maximum static friction is twenty newtons.

00:04:09.348 --> 00:04:10.708
Now push with twelve newtons.

00:04:11.388 --> 00:04:11.848
Will it move?

00:04:12.908 --> 00:04:14.568
Pause and compare twelve with the limit.

00:04:15.608 --> 00:04:19.388
Static friction can supply twelve newtons in the opposite direction, so the

00:04:19.388 --> 00:04:20.088
block stays at rest.

00:04:20.648 --> 00:04:24.068
Its actual friction is twelve, not twenty.

00:04:24.068 --> 00:04:26.228
Next increase the push to twenty eight newtons.

00:04:26.228 --> 00:04:31.588
Rest is no longer possible because the required friction exceeds twenty.

00:04:31.588 --> 00:04:36.028
Once the block slides, use kinetic friction: zero point three times forty

00:04:36.028 --> 00:04:37.068
equals twelve newtons.

00:04:38.188 --> 00:04:41.428
The net force is twenty eight minus twelve, or sixteen newtons.

00:04:42.148 --> 00:04:45.728
Divide by four kilograms and the acceleration is four metres per second

00:04:45.728 --> 00:04:46.068
squared.

00:04:47.408 --> 00:04:50.508
Friction opposes relative slipping at the contact, or its tendency.

00:04:51.228 --> 00:04:56.008
It does not always oppose the body's overall motion: static friction can push a

00:04:56.008 --> 00:04:56.888
walking person forward.

00:04:57.888 --> 00:05:01.128
Always identify the contact and its slipping tendency first.

00:05:01.416 --> 00:05:04.896
On a slope, a better choice of axes makes the problem simpler.

00:05:05.756 --> 00:05:08.956
Choose one axis down the slope and another perpendicular to it.

00:05:09.636 --> 00:05:11.476
Weight still points vertically downward.

00:05:12.116 --> 00:05:17.156
Its component down the slope is m g sine theta, and its component into the

00:05:17.156 --> 00:05:21.836
slope is m g cosine theta, where theta is the incline's angle to the horizontal.

00:05:22.716 --> 00:05:26.636
The normal reaction balances the perpendicular component if no other

00:05:26.636 --> 00:05:29.696
perpendicular forces act and there is no perpendicular acceleration.

00:05:30.176 --> 00:05:32.896
It therefore equals m g cosine theta.

00:05:33.756 --> 00:05:37.376
Let us solve a two kilogram block sliding on a frictionless thirty degree

00:05:37.376 --> 00:05:39.416
incline, using g equal to ten.

00:05:40.236 --> 00:05:42.696
First predict whether the acceleration depends on mass.

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:48.146
The downslope force is two times ten times one

00:05:48.146 --> 00:05:51.276
half, or ten newtons.

00:05:51.916 --> 00:05:55.496
Divide by two kilograms to get five metres per second squared down the slope.

00:05:56.256 --> 00:05:56.876
Mass cancels.

00:05:56.876 --> 00:06:01.536
The normal reaction is twenty times cosine thirty degrees, which is ten times

00:06:01.536 --> 00:06:04.396
the square root of three newtons, about seventeen point three.

00:06:05.056 --> 00:06:07.436
It is perpendicular to the slope, not vertical.

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:12.156
If the block slides downward with kinetic friction, subtract mu k times the

00:06:12.156 --> 00:06:15.456
normal reaction from the downslope force before dividing by mass.

00:06:16.136 --> 00:06:19.396
An upslope acceleration can slow a block that is still moving downward.

00:06:20.536 --> 00:06:25.096
Finish every force problem with four checks: one body, clear axes, correct

00:06:25.096 --> 00:06:27.136
contact model, and consistent units.

00:06:28.036 --> 00:06:31.336
Complete the practice questions next, and keep the written lesson open for the

00:06:31.336 --> 00:06:31.876
worked solutions.
